even  be looking at the baltic states which we  have an obligation to defend  so i think it is prudent to take steps  now to assist the ukrainians as they  have an organized government and  military and are fighting back rather  than waiting until it is disorganized in  ukraine and putin is in a dominant  position and moving on to other uh  pieces of the empire  let me ask you uh  i guess a question that i put you in our  previous interview if  we don't for example introduce a no-fly  zone  is it your view that ultimately the  ukrainians are going to have to submit  to russian control  no  i think the ukrainians are going to  continue fighting  and even if  the russians manage to kill zilinski and  even if they manage to break the  military organization  then the ukrainians will be fighting  from the cities and the woods uh in an  internal insurgency against a russian  occupying force this is going to go on  uh regardless  so this is potentially what our own  foreign secretary  called before the invasion the potential  for a quagmire for the russians um i  think she was  calling to bind uh afghanistan  maybe the first chechen war yeah the  difference between a quagmire as we  would understand it  and what would be happening in ukraine  under those circumstances is the  russians are absolutely brutal they  won't play by any rules of nicety they  will kill civilians indiscriminately  they will be incredibly brutal and that  is again another reason we should be  looking at ways to stop this before it  happens  um let me just ask lastly one thing  which as a former official  you've served under a number of  presidents  including president trump  it's been said to me actually on  television this week  that were president trump in the white  house mr putin would never have dared  uh to be so  audacious so brutal  so uh aggressive  do you agree with that  it's hard to know  it's hard to know  the um i think there is an element to  which president trump was highly  unpredictable  and i think putin was  uh not sure what trump would do  uh i also think that he believed that  trump was comfortable using force look  at the way we took out the head of the  iranian revolutionary guard  uh or uh you know pushed back um  in syria and against isis you know there  was a there was a bit of aggressiveness  in the approach  which i think putin saw and that also  increased uncertainty  but that being said  uh putin is bent on this mission to  rebuild the russian empire  on the ashes of the former soviet union  he wants to regather the territories  he's been explicit about it and so  i think his being driven to do this  would be the case whether it was  president trump or president biden
 

